(1).JSX
->JSX (JavaScript XML) is a syntax extension for JavaScript, commonly used with the React library to describe the structure of user interfaces. 
->JSX allows developers to write HTML-like markup directly within JavaScript code.
* HTML-like syntax: 
JSX provides a familiar syntax similar to HTML, making it intuitive for web developers to define UI elements.
* Embeddable JavaScript expressions:
You can embed any valid JavaScript expression within curly braces {} inside JSX.
This enables dynamic content rendering, including variables, function calls, and more. 

(2).Super powers of JSX
->JavaScript XML, offers several powerful features that make building user interfaces in React more intuitive and efficient.
->JSX allows developers to describe the structure of the UI using a syntax that closely resembles HTML, making it highly readable and understandable.
-> JSX enables the embedding of JavaScript expressions directly within the markup using curly braces {}.
->JSX inherently supports and encourages a component-based approach to UI development.

(3).Role of Type attribute
->The type attribute in an HTML <script> tag specifies the Internet media type (formerly known as MIME type) of the script. 
->This attribute provides context to browsers on how the code within the <script> tags should be handled and executed. 
type of  attribute i can use 
->This is the traditional and default MIME type for classic JavaScript scripts.
If the type attribute is omitted, browsers will typically assume text/javascript.
While still widely used, modern recommendations suggest omitting the attribute for standard JavaScript to encourage browser optimization

(4).Tittle component
->In JSX, the syntax for rendering components and passing children differs significantly: {TitleComponent}.
->This syntax is used for embedding JavaScript expressions within JSX.
-> If TitleComponent were a variable holding a string, number, or another valid JSX element, that value would be rendered. 
->If TitleComponent were a function that returns JSX, calling that function (e.g., {TitleComponent()}) would render its output.

